---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout section="OAuth 2.0" title="OAuth 2.0 Fundamentals">
  <div class="page-wrap">
    <div class="learn-layout">
      <nav class="toc">
        <div class="toc-label">On this page</div>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#problem">The Problem OAuth Solves</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-is">What is OAuth 2.0?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#actors">The Four Actors</a></li>
          <li><a href="#api-keys-vs-oauth">API Keys vs OAuth Tokens</a></li>
          <li><a href="#scopes">Scopes &amp; Consent</a></li>
          <li><a href="#clients">Public vs Confidential Clients</a></li>
          <li><a href="#channels">Front Channel vs Back Channel</a></li>
          <li><a href="#framework-variance">Framework vs Protocol Variance</a></li>
          <li><a href="#endpoints">OAuth Endpoints</a></li>
          <li><a href="#security">Web Security 101 Checklist</a></li>
        </ul>
        <hr class="toc-divider" />
        <nav class="toc-nav">
          <a href={base + '/'}>Home</a>
          <a href={base + '/flows'}>Grant Types &rarr;</a>
        </nav>
      </nav>

      <main class="content">
        <div class="page-header">
          <span class="badge badge-beginner">Beginner &middot; 20 min</span>
          <h1>OAuth 2.0 Fundamentals</h1>
          <p class="lead">Understanding delegated authorization, the password anti-pattern, actor topologies, token consumers, and modern security best practices under RFC 6749 and RFC 9700.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <section id="problem">
          <h2>The Problem OAuth Solves</h2>
          <p>Before OAuth, if a third-party application needed access to a user's data on another service (e.g., printing contacts or managing calendars), it asked the user for their raw username and password. This is known as the <strong>password anti-pattern</strong>.</p>
          <div class="callout danger">
            <strong>⚠️ Hazards of the Password Anti-Pattern:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Over-Privileged Access:</strong> The third-party application receives full access to the user's entire account.</li>
              <li><strong>Irrevocability:</strong> The user cannot revoke access from a single app without changing their master password.</li>
              <li><strong>Credential Compromise:</strong> If the third-party application is breached, the user's primary password is leaked.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- WHAT IS -->
        <section id="what-is">
          <h2>What is OAuth 2.0?</h2>
          <p>According to <strong>RFC 6749</strong>:</p>
          <blockquote>
            "The OAuth 2.0 authorization framework enables a third-party application to obtain limited access to an HTTP service, either on behalf of a resource owner by orchestrating an approval interaction between the resource owner and the HTTP service, or by allowing the third-party application to obtain access on its own behalf."
          </blockquote>
          <p>A classic analogy is the <strong>valet key</strong> for an automobile. A valet key grants the parking attendant permission to start the engine and drive the vehicle, but does not open the trunk or glovebox, and is returned after use.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ACTORS -->
        <section id="actors">
          <h2>The Four OAuth Actors (RFC 6749 Section 1.1)</h2>
          <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:1rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">1. Resource Owner (The User)</h3>
              <p>An entity capable of granting access to a protected resource (e.g., the end-user).</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">2. Resource Server (The Protected API)</h3>
              <p>The server hosting protected user resources that validates and accepts OAuth Bearer tokens.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">3. Client (The Application)</h3>
              <p>The application making protected resource requests on behalf of the Resource Owner.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">4. Authorization Server (Identity Provider)</h3>
              <p>The server issuing access tokens to the Client after successfully authenticating the Resource Owner and obtaining authorization.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- API KEYS VS OAUTH -->
        <section id="api-keys-vs-oauth">
          <h2>API Keys vs OAuth 2.0 Tokens</h2>
          <p>Why static API keys fail for enterprise applications compared to OAuth tokens:</p>
          <pre><code><span class="c">// 1. Static API Key Request (Insecure: static, broad, no user context)</span>
GET /api/reports HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
X-API-Key: secret_live_abcdef123456789

<span class="c">// 2. OAuth 2.0 Bearer Token Request (Secure: scoped, short-lived, signed)</span>
GET /api/reports HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...</code></pre>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Security Dimension</th><th>Static API Key</th><th>OAuth 2.0 Bearer Token</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>User Context</strong></td><td>None (Application-level only)</td><td>Bound to specific user (<code>sub</code> claim)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Lifetime</strong></td><td>Long-lived / Indefinite</td><td>Short-lived (15–60 mins)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Revocation</strong></td><td>Hard (Requires code redeploy)</td><td>Instantaneous via token revocation or expiration</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Scopes</strong></td><td>Coarse / All-or-nothing</td><td>Granular permissions (<code>read:reports</code>)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- SCOPES -->
        <section id="scopes">
          <h2>OAuth Scopes &amp; Consent</h2>
          <p>Scopes are space-separated strings defining the boundaries of requested access. During authorization, the user is presented with a consent screen enumerating these permissions.</p>
          <div class="callout note">
            <strong>Common Examples:</strong>
            <ul>
              <li><code>read:users write:users</code> (Custom API permissions)</li>
              <li><code>openid profile email</code> (OIDC standard scopes)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CLIENTS -->
        <section id="clients">
          <h2>Public vs Confidential Clients</h2>
          <p>RFC 6749 categorizes clients by their ability to keep credentials confidential:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Confidential Clients:</strong> Server-side web applications running in secure backend environments (Node.js, Java, Python). They can safely maintain a <code>client_secret</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Public Clients:</strong> Single Page Apps (React, Vue) or Native Mobile Apps (iOS, Android). The source code and binary can be decompiled; <strong>they can NEVER keep a secret</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="callout danger">
            <strong>⚠️ Security Rule:</strong> Never distribute a <code>client_secret</code> in client-side JavaScript or an app distributed through public App Stores. Use <strong>Authorization Code + PKCE</strong> instead.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CHANNELS -->
        <section id="channels">
          <h2>Front Channel vs Back Channel</h2>
          <p>OAuth 2.0 segregates interactions across two distinct network paths:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Front Channel:</strong> Carried out via user browser redirects (e.g., <code>GET /authorize</code>). Visible in browser history and logs; must only carry transient authorization codes, never raw tokens or client secrets.</li>
            <li><strong>Back Channel:</strong> Direct server-to-server TLS encrypted HTTPS POST requests (e.g., <code>POST /token</code>). Used for exchanging codes for access tokens and validating credentials.</li>
          </ul>

          <pre class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor RO as 👤 Resource Owner (User)
    participant C as 📱 Client App (SPA / Web / Mobile)
    participant AS as 🔑 Authorization Server (IdP)
    participant RS as 🗄️ Resource Server (API)

    Note over RO,AS: 🌐 FRONT CHANNEL (Browser Redirects / Visible)
    RO-&gt;&gt;C: 1. Initiate Action / Login
    C-&gt;&gt;AS: 2. Redirect to GET /authorize (scope, state, client_id)
    RO-&gt;&gt;AS: 3. Authenticate &amp; Grant Consent
    AS--&gt;&gt;C: 4. 302 Redirect to redirect_uri with code &amp; state

    Note over C,RS: 🔒 BACK CHANNEL (Direct TLS Encrypted HTTPS POST)
    C-&gt;&gt;AS: 5. POST /token (grant_type=authorization_code, code, client_secret/PKCE)
    AS--&gt;&gt;C: 6. 200 OK (access_token, id_token, refresh_token)
    C-&gt;&gt;RS: 7. GET /api/data (Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token&gt;)
    RS--&gt;&gt;C: 8. 200 OK (Protected JSON Payload)
          </pre>
        </section>

        <!-- FRAMEWORK VARIANCE -->
        <section id="framework-variance">
          <h2>OAuth 2.0: Framework vs Protocol Variance</h2>
          <div class="callout warning">
            <strong>⚠️ Architectural Variance &amp; Normalization:</strong>
            <p>OAuth 2.0 is defined as an <em>Authorization Framework</em> rather than a rigid protocol. As a result, vendors (Okta, Microsoft Entra ID, Ping, Auth0) implement parameter options, token payload formats, and role mappings differently.</p>
            <p>Enterprise applications and API Gateways (such as Azure APIM) must implement <strong>claim normalization adapters</strong> to map vendor-specific claims (e.g. Okta <code>groups</code> vs Entra <code>roles</code>) into a unified permission model.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ENDPOINTS -->
        <section id="endpoints">
          <h2>OAuth 2.0 Endpoints (RFC 6749, RFC 7662, RFC 7009)</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><code>/authorize</code>: Front-channel endpoint for user authentication and consent.</li>
            <li><code>/token</code>: Back-channel endpoint for code and refresh token exchanges.</li>
            <li><code>/introspect</code> (RFC 7662): Resource server endpoint to verify token status and metadata.</li>
            <li><code>/revoke</code> (RFC 7009): Endpoint for explicit client-side token revocation.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- SECURITY CHECKLIST -->
        <section id="security">
          <h2>Web Security 101 Checklist (RFC 9700)</h2>
          <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.5rem; margin-top:1rem;">
            <div>&#9989; <strong>Always use <code>state</code>:</strong> Cryptographic random string to guarantee flow integrity and defeat CSRF.</div>
            <div>&#9989; <strong>Always use PKCE:</strong> Proof Key for Code Exchange (RFC 7636) for public and confidential clients.</div>
            <div>&#9989; <strong>Strict Redirect URI Whitelisting:</strong> Validate exact URL matches to prevent token harvesting redirects.</div>
            <div>&#9989; <strong>Bind <code>client_id</code>:</strong> Ensure token requests bind the grant code to the same client ID.</div>
            <div>&#9989; <strong>Short Token Lifetimes:</strong> Use 15–60 minute access tokens with refresh token rotation.</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <footer class="page-footer">
          <div class="prev-next">
            <span></span>
            <a href={base + '/flows'}>Next: Grant Types &rarr;</a>
          </div>
        </footer>
      </main>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
